From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: WAR/NANO/LAW:  Against Nanarchy

Hal Finney:
>In the same way, I don't think it makes sense to hold human "parents" always
>responsible for the actions of their AI "children".  

We come up against a major problem of standards.  Today ages 16-21
are typical for "adulthood" of various legal forms (driver's
license, voting age, drinking age, etc.)  But AIs can be highly
customized.   One sort of AI might take centuries
to evolve into a god-like being that recycles its own material
into ever more faster computing substrate.  Another kind of AI is 
the kamicopy, a mayfly ninja sent out to perform its unprecedented, 
unpredictable coercive deed and then self-destruct, leaving no trace 
of its maker.  Or we might be combine the two: a centuries-long plot
to topple nanarchy by beaming our trojan horse gods, bred for centuries
to a point of immense logical depth, into the nearby star systems.
The petty, paltry defenses conceived quadrillions of Cray-millenia
ago would be rubbed out like bugs on a windshield.  Unless the 
defenses, too, had evolved: in which case the outcome of the battle 
would be quite unpredictable a century before.

> It depends on how
> recently you created the AI, the initial state you created it in, what you
> taught it, and so on.  In some cases you would be, and in other cases it
> would not make sense for you to be.  If you created the AI twenty years
> ago and it has been living on its own all this time, I don't think it
> would make any more sense to hold you responsible for its actions than for
> the actions of your grown human children.

But does one's control over the AI necessarily lessen over time?

Robin Hanson:
> > Sure, "whoever expands fastest ... gets to have more
> > mass-energy at his disposal to beat on the other guys".  

This might be, well, immaterial.  A better strategy could be 
to spend cycles on increasing computing mem*cycles with current materials,
rather than obtaining new raw materials in distant places.  Many
variables -- speed of light lag, physical limits to computation,
the memory-time cost of reversibility vs. the memory-time cost
of building more radiator surface, etc.  Some strategies will
lead to better cryptanalysis, trojan horse capabilities, physical
weapons, and/or economic capabilities within the
market than competitors which choose a different brain/brawn
and weapons mix.  The chance that we can find, in this practically 
infinite search space, the best mix for implementing the nano-Gorts,
is practically nil.

> Perhaps we could agree that, given enough of a head start, one agent 
> effectively take permanent possession of all non-Solar star systems.

I find it hard to imagine competitive agents with lag times over
a few seconds.  As computating speed grows, even lags of nanoseconds
might become prohibitive.  As computing mem*cycles grow, agents may
become more distant from each other in subjective lag time, without 
becoming more distant physically.  We might get the "interstellar peace 
effect" with billions of such posthuman agents here on Earth.

> I for one don't buy Miller's argument that owning 10^-10 of the universe
> is 5% as good as owning the entire universe.

The fraction of the universe owned will likely be unimportant
compared to how well one can put it to use.

Initial definition of property rights raises many of the same
bound-rationality problems as central planning of economies.
Only much worse: in this case the planners must forecast
both future technology changes and the actions of agents 
not just as smart as themselves, but quadrillions of times
smarter.  Imagine, for example, defining property rights
for the radio spectrum in the 18th century. 

