Date: Wed, 1 Sep 1993 12:41:11 -0400
From: tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Subject: AIT:Socialist computation

Robin Hanson wrote:
>This complexity argument against central planning picks too easy a
>target.  Sure a central planning board couldn't hope to decide when
>everyone eats lunch and goes to bed, and whether your curtains should
>be blue or white.  No one argues they should plan *everything*.

That's not what is being argued. The idea is, the planning board has a
ranking for consumption goods, they know whether an additional lunch is
more socially valuable than an additional set of curtains; but they have to
decide how to arrange production so that relatively little is wasted.
Should more iron ore be mined, or should it be left in the ground a while
longer? What process should be used to extract it? What process should
refine it? What industries should use it as raw material? 

The socialists wanted to gloss over these questions, assuming that such
engineering questions were strictly questions of science and technique, not
valuation. But prices of machinery and raw materials are the connection
between production processes and outputs. Science alone will not tell you
the best process to use in producing an item. There are many alternative
processes for making identical goods or goods that can be used for the same
purpose. Science alone might suggest that gold is the best material for
electical wire, but there's not enough to go around. Socialists can ration
it, but will the rationing correspond to their ranking of consumer goods
better than some alternative? Will it mean too many or too few personal
computers for their taste?

>The harder target to argue against is the idea that central planners
>could be well enough "calibrated" to know when they know enough to
>intervene, and when they don't know enough, and so should back off and
>leave things alone.  

In full socialism, there is nothing to intervene into. Since no one owns
any productive property, all production decisions are ultimately up to the
planners.
They can routinize or delegate decisions, and then intervene if they don't
like the result, but this is more like changing their minds than
intervening in an independent process. The question then becomes, how do
the routines or the delegates make the connection between the planners'
rankings their decisions without prices and profits to guide them?

>Imagine we created an AI, optimized not so much to be really smart,
>but to be well-calibrated, to understand to the concept economic
>efficiency, and to have such efficiency as a goal.  Then imagine
>giving it absolute power over us.

Such an AI would not (IMHO) achieve the socialists' dream. It would be a
complex system, equilvalent to the market (if it could be made to work at
all). It would be beyond human comprehension, so even if it worked and did
not abuse its power (beyond the abuse of human freedom such tyranny would
require), you still likely get business cycles and unemployment. I think
Mises and Hayek would say that the most likely method of implementing such
a device would be to have intelligent subagents of the AI owning,
investing, buying, and selling. The only thing it would achieve would be
egalitarian monetary income.

>The best arguments against such an AI king, to my mind, would question
>our ability to supervise the construction of an AI we all accept as
>neutrally calibrated, and the risk that someone/thing else will take
>over this supposed "abolute" power.

I think the socialists' response to this would be, 'why give the AI
absolute power?' Why not just make its decisions advisory, with nice
democratic institutions mediating between the plan and human rights? Of
course, then you might have to give people incentives to work, which blows
away the egalitarian monetary income. Not much left to call socialist, is
there?

